@@ -23,4 +23,12 @@ Resolve the immutable source revision before rendering. The cache key includes c
 
 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+Use 2Q cache replacement to protect sheets repeatedly used during comparison sessions while adapting quickly when editors switch projects. The cache maintains two queues: A1 (first-access entries) and A2 (reused entries). When a cached entry is accessed, promote it from A1 to A2. On eviction pressure, remove entries from A1 first; only if A1 is empty, evict from A2.
+
+The queue-size split is configurable but defaults to: A1 occupies 25% of the 12 GiB cache, A2 occupies 75%. This ratio can be tuned per production patterns after pilot deployment. Entries move to A2 after a single reaccess; entries remain in A1 across an entire screening pass without being accessed twice.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |
+|----------|-----------|-------------------------|
+| 2Q cache replacement | Protects comparison-session working sets (A2) from eviction during sequential screenings (A1), while maintaining quick adaptation to project switches via A1 turnover. After three meetings evaluating LRU, frequency-with-aging, and 2Q, 2Q was selected for this balance: it requires modest bookkeeping (two queue pointers per entry, two queue heads), resists the sequential-scan vulnerability that affects LRU, and avoids the counter staleness risk of aged frequency tracking. The 25%/75% split reserves quarter capacity for one-off sheets discovered during screenings while allocating most capacity to repeated comparisons. | LRU: minimal bookkeeping and immediate adaptation, but long sequential screenings evict useful comparison sheets. Frequency-based with aging: protects repeated clips, but counter maintenance and aging decay add complexity; can retain yesterday's favorites too long after a project switch. |
